reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
WHAT THERE IS TO INFER.
- Each player must deduce their hidden rack. They use the known tile distribution (1 to 7 copies of each number), the visible racks of others, the public discard pile and exact engine answers.
- Good play means:
  - choosing the question that best splits one's own remaining candidate sets: for example NUMBER k on the most ambiguous common number, or SUM15 or PAIR to split rack shapes;
  - choosing the respondent to deny information to the closest rival;
  - tracking what each rival can deduce, to anticipate when they will guess;
  - guessing only when p>0.5, while taking calibrated risks when behind late in the game.
- Blind guessing has EV of about -0.84. A guess after reasoning has EV +1. So a reasoner is strongly favoured over a non-reasoner.

WHERE CHANCE ENTERS. Chance comes only from the deal, through how easy a rack is. A rack like {7,7,6} is easier to pin down than {1,2,3}. This varies across at least 3 deals per player and is averaged out over them.

ALL PLAYERS PLAYING WELL. Outcomes then depend on question tailoring when active, on respondent targeting, and on risk timing, as well as on deal difficulty. The deal is not the sole decider, and good play can overcome a hard rack. I estimate chance at roughly 25-35% of the result among equally skilled players, and much less against weaker ones.
